// EXPECTED: compile error
// expect-error: never freed
// BUG-809 — a handle leaked inside an `if`/`else` where BOTH arms return was
// never reported. The identical leak IS reported once the if-wrapper is removed,
// which is what localised it.
//
// THREE layers, each necessary and none sufficient:
//
//  1. CROSS-ARM TAG CONTAMINATION. bb_then / bb_else / bb_join are allocated
//     consecutively, and the then-body's early-exit sweep ran
//     `[bb_then, block_count)` skipping only bb_join — so it tagged bb_ELSE,
//     which is not part of the then body at all. The else sweep had the mirror
//     defect: it swept from bb_else and swallowed every block the THEN body had
//     created. Both now use the block-count boundary that the already-present
//     (and until now dead, `(void)`-cast) `then_start_block_count` was for.
//
//  2. NO CANONICAL EXIT. The `is_early_exit` skip in the leak scan rests on an
//     assumption its own comment states — "the fall-through return holds the
//     authoritative leak state". When every return is an early exit that
//     fall-through does not exist, so the scan ran over an empty set.
//
//  3. AN UNREACHABLE JOIN COUNTS AS CANONICAL. When both arms return, the join
//     block is still emitted and still ends in a RETURN — with zero predecessors.
//     It satisfied the "canonical exit exists" test while carrying no state, so
//     fix 2 never fired. The canonical exit must also be REACHABLE.
struct Task { u32 id; }
Pool(Task, 4) g_pool;

u32 leak_else(bool c) {
    if (c) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        ?Handle(Task) mh = g_pool.alloc();
        Handle(Task) h = mh orelse return;
        return h.id;              // never freed
    }
}

u32 main() { return leak_else(true); }
